  "merits": "These are the etiquettes of travel with which Shaykh Abbas al-Qummi opens the chapter on ziyarat in Mafatih al-Jinan. The etiquettes at the shrine itself are in [Etiquettes of Ziyarat](B3).\n\nThe days to avoid: Mafatih gives the seven ill-omened days of each month in two Persian couplets: the 3rd, 5th, 13th, 16th, 21st, 24th and 25th.\n\nCharity on setting out: a companion of Imam al-Baqir (a.s.) came to bid him farewell before a journey. The Imam told him, “Whenever my father, Ali ibn al-Husayn (a.s.), set out to see to his property, he bought his safety from Allah with whatever charity he could give, and he gave it as he put his foot in the stirrup. When Allah brought him back safely, he thanked and praised Allah, again with whatever charity he could give.” The man left without doing as he was told, and perished on the way. When the news reached Imam al-Baqir (a.s.), he said, “He was advised; had he accepted the advice, he would have been saved.”\n\nThe almond staff: the Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) said, “Whoever sets out on a journey with a staff of bitter almond wood and recites, ‘And when he turned his face towards Midian...’ to ‘...and Allah is a witness over what we say’, Allah keeps him safe from every harmful beast, every thieving robber and every venomous creature until he returns home, and seventy-seven angels accompany him, asking forgiveness for him, until he returns and puts the staff down.”\n\nThe rings: in Aman al-Akhtar, Sayyid Ibn Tawus reports from Abu Muhammad Qasim ibn al-Ala' that Safi, the servant of Imam Ali al-Hadi (a.s.), said: “I asked his leave to go on ziyarat to Tus. He told me, ‘Take with you a ring whose stone is yellow agate, engraved with “Ma sha' Allah, la quwwata illa billah, astaghfirullah” on one side and “Muhammad and Ali” on the other. It is a protection from highway robbery, the more certain safety, and the better guard of your religion.’ I went out, had a ring made as he described, and came back to bid him farewell. I had gone some way when he had me called back, and said, ‘Safi, take another ring with you, of turquoise. Between Tus and Nishapur a lion will meet you and stop the caravan. Go up to it, show it the ring and say, “My master tells you: move off the road.” Let its engraving be “Allah al-Malik”, and on the other side “al-Mulk lillah al-Wahid al-Qahhar”. That was the ring of Amir al-Mu'minin (a.s.): it was engraved “Allah al-Malik”, and when he took up the caliphate he engraved on it “al-Mulk lillah al-Wahid al-Qahhar”. Its stone was turquoise, and it is a protection from wild beasts especially, and victory in war.’\n\n“I set out on that journey, and by Allah the lion met me. I did as I was told, and when I came back I told him about it. He said, ‘There is one thing left you have not told me; if you wish, I will tell you.’ I said, ‘My master, tell me; perhaps I have forgotten it.’ He said, ‘Yes. You spent a night in Tus by the grave, and a party of jinn came to the grave to visit it. They saw the stone on your hand and read its engraving, took it from your hand and carried it to a sick one of theirs, washed the ring in water and gave him the water to drink, and he was cured. Then they returned the ring to you; it had been on your right hand, and they put it on your left. You wondered greatly at this and did not know why. And you found by your head a stone, a ruby, and took it, and it is with you. Take it to the market: you will sell it for eighty dinars. It is the jinn's gift to you.’ I took it to the market and sold it for eighty dinars, as my master had said.”\n\n[Ayat al-Kursi](A4): Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said, “Whoever recites [Ayat al-Kursi](A4) every night on a journey is kept safe, and what is with him is kept safe.”\n\nImam Zayn al-Abidin (a.s.) said that once he had said these words (“In the Name of Allah, and by Allah...”), he would not care if jinn and men together set upon him.\n\nOn the way: Shaykh Abbas al-Qummi writes that the duas and etiquettes of travel are many, and lists a few. It is narrated that keeping one's provisions safe is part of a traveller's understanding. Whoever helps his companions on a journey and meets their needs, Allah relieves him of seventy-three hardships, shields him in this world from worry and grief, and relieves his great distress on the Day of Resurrection. Imam Zayn al-Abidin (a.s.) would travel only with companions who did not know him, so that he could serve them on the way; those who knew him would not let him. And it was of the noble character of the Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) that, on a journey with his companions, when they wanted to slaughter a sheep, one said, “I will slaughter it,” another, “I will skin it,” and another, “I will cook it,” and he said, “I will gather the firewood.” They said, “O Messenger of Allah, we will do it; do not trouble yourself.” He replied, “I know that you would do it, but I do not like to set myself apart from you. Allah dislikes to see His servant set himself above his companions.” The heaviest of people on his companions in a journey is the one who is idle though sound in body, does nothing of his share, and waits for his companions to see to his needs.\n\nOf forbearance, more is said in the etiquettes of the journey to [Imam Husayn (a.s.)](AG2), and there too of plain food on that journey. Ibn al-A'sam put the etiquette of provisions in verse: «مِنْ شَرَفِ الْاِنْسَانِ فِي الْاَسْفَارِ تَطْيِيْبُهُ الزَّادَ مَعَ الْاِكْثَارِ، وَلْيُحْسِنِ الْاِنْسَانُ فِيْ حَالِ السَّفَرِ اَخْلَاقَهٗ زِيَادَةً عَلَى الْحَضَرِ، وَلْيَدْعُ عِنْدَ الْوَضْعِ لِلْخِوَانِ مَنْ كَانَ حَاضِرًا مِنَ الْاِخْوَانِ، وَلْيُكْثِرِ الْمَزْحَ مَعَ الصَّحْبِ اِذَا لَمْ يُسْخِطِ اللّٰهَ وَلَمْ يَجْلِبْ اَذٰى، مَنْ جَاۤءَ بَلْدَةً فَذَا ضَيْفٌ عَلٰى اِخْوَانِهٖ فِيْهَاۤ اِلٰۤى اَنْ يَرْحَلَا، يُبَرُّ لَيْلَتَيْنِ ثُمَّ لْيَاْكُلِ مِنْ اَكْلِ اَهْلِ الْبَيْتِ فِي الْمُسْتَقْبِلِ» (“It is part of a man's honour on journeys to take good provisions, and plenty of them; on a journey let him be better in character than at home; when the meal is laid, let him invite whichever brothers are present; let him joke often with his companions, so long as it does not anger Allah or bring harm; whoever comes to a town is the guest of his brothers there until he departs: he is honoured for two nights, and after that he eats what the household eats”).\n\nPrayer on the journey: the most important thing on a journey is to keep the obligatory prayers, with their conditions and limits, at the start of their times. How often pilgrims on the road are seen to neglect them, delaying them past their time, praying them riding or in their litters, with tayammum and no wudu, or with impure body or clothes. All of this comes from taking prayer lightly. Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said, “An obligatory prayer is better than twenty hajjs, and one hajj is better than a house full of gold given away in charity until it is empty.” The thirty tasbihs after a shortened prayer are among the emphasised sunnahs."
